.TH mkfs "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
mkfs \- make a new file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/mkfs \fIspecial proto\fR
.br
\fB/etc/mkfs [\-b \fIboot\fB] [\-f \fIfname\fB] [\-p \fIpack\fB] [\-i \fIn\fB] [\-n \fIn\fB] [\-m \fIn\fB] \fIspecial n\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mkfs -- make a new file system
Usage:	/etc/mkfs special proto
	/etc/mkfs [ -b boot ] [ -f fname ] [ -p pack ] [ -i n ] [ -n n ] [ -m n ] special n
Options:
	-b boot	Name of the boot block file
	-f fname	File system name to record in the label
	-p pack	File pack name to record in the label
	-i n	Number of i-nodes
	-n n	Free-list interleave factor n
	-m n	Free-list interleave factor m
If 'proto' is a number, it is the size in blocks of an empty file system;
otherwise, it is a prototype description file.
.HE
.B mkfs
writes a new file system onto
.I special
(normally a block special file).
It has two forms.
In the first,
.I proto
names a prototype description file that lists the boot file, the
file system's name and pack name, its size and number of i-nodes,
its bad blocks, and the directories and files it is to contain;
.B mkfs
reads this file to build the file system.
In the second,
.I n
is the size of the file system in blocks, and
.B mkfs
constructs an empty file system of that size (holding nothing but a
root directory) using values taken from the option letters below,
in place of a prototype file; a
.I proto
that is entirely digits selects this form, anything else selects the
first form.
If any of the option letters are given together with a real
.I proto
file, they are superfluous and
.B mkfs
warns and ignores them.
.IP "\fB\-b\ \fIboot\fR"
Name of the file holding the bootstrap to be written into block 0
(default: no boot file).
.IP "\fB\-f\ \fIfname\fR"
Name to store in the file system label (default: \fBnoname\fR).
.IP "\fB\-p\ \fIpack\fR"
Pack (device) name to store in the file system label (default:
\fBnopack\fR).
.IP "\fB\-i\ \fIn\fR"
Number of i-nodes to create (default: about one i-node per five to
seven blocks, depending on file system size).
.IP "\fB\-n\ \fIn\fR"
Free-list interleave factor
.I n
(numerator); together with
.B \-m
controls how successive free blocks are spread across the disk.
Default is 1.
.IP "\fB\-m\ \fIn\fR"
Free-list interleave factor
.I n
(denominator).
Default is 1.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mount ,
.BR mknod ,
.B mkproto
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mkfs
complains and stops if it cannot open
.I special
or
.IR proto ,
if
.I proto
is malformed (a missing or out-of-range value, a badly formed file
specification line, or a premature end of file), or if a regular
file named in
.I proto
cannot be read or has changed size since it was measured; it prints
its two-line usage message if called with the wrong number of
arguments.
.SH NOTES
Options are recognized only in the second form, immediately after the
command name and before
.I special
and
.IR n .
